#!/usr/bin/env bash

lord --verbose << "EOF"
I've added nav sections throughout main.tex to clarify goals

I want you to look at the new organization and try to use it
as a template for how to refactor the code we have so far in
order to simplify the implementation and improve readability
and maintainability and usability of the code for the future

The more we can preseve what we already have while making it
simpler in its implementation, the easier it will be to move
quickly and modify things in whatever unforseen ways we will
have to in the near and distant future. Now I want you to...

1.  Delete the things marked as deprecated from both main.tex
    and from the rest of the book.
2.  Move the implementations of various \def's, \newcommand's
    \NewDocumentCommand's, \DeclareRobustCommand's, & all the
    various things like that into the part of the nav-rrative
    where they belong. The nav is organized like a narrative,
    and I don't want you to introduce anything into a part in
    which it doesn't belong. This will help us keep things in
    the right level of abstraction, decoupled from stuff they
    have no business coupling with. For example, you will see
    the stuff in the tower of babel section of the preamble's
    all about languages and writing systems themselves, while
    the patriarchal history section is about the authors: who
    wrote the bible, what groups they belonged to, and defn's
    to make it easier to refer to their fonts, bg & fg colors
    or the attributes of groups to which they belong, like in
    our (first drafts of) the \Levite, \Aaronid, and \Mushite
    commands that exist to help force the book's decisions to
    be principled, by giving a meaning to every color & every
    font weight & every font attribute both of the Hebrew and
    of the English. That said, we need to be able to override
    attributes or change implementations easy, if for example
    P's Hebrew font is already bold looking by default and so
    making it bold with the \Levite command is visually a bit
    too much. That's ok, because if we have the correct level
    of abstraction for all the primitives in the book then we
    can just change P's Hebrew Boldness by removing '\Levite'
    from one place and nowhere else, and then adding comments
    saying "P's Hebrew font is already bold, so we're already
    obeying the rules. By making MOST things programmatic and
    yet still making the implementations easy to change, weve
    got the best of both worlds.

With that in mind, read main.tex, look at what I'm aiming for,
and use what I've just told you to reimplement the entire file
as dramatically and totally as you would at your highest level
of ambition and care. Feel free to start completely from first
principles, start from scratch, and reimplement everything you
think could be made better. Store the results in a file called
master.tex, and switch the Makefile to use master.tex, instead
of main.tex, and continue to refer to main.tex while you write
master.tex. We'll iterate until the system is much better, and
simpler, with fewer moving parts and hard coded names, and all
the same functionality from fewer functions called lotsa times
in the definitions of other functions, so the result will be a
lot smaller and yet much easier to change in one-off ways when
exceptions and special cases arise, like the case of Ps hebrew
font weight above. Also make sure to add back the niqqud to Ps
Hebrew while you're there. Have fun and be ambitious...  first
principles and elegance or bust.

-J
EOF
